<p id="p2"><a href="https://pingie.com/homesnap" rel="noopener noreferrer">HomeSnap</a> remembers everything your HomeKit home does, shows you its live status, and tells you the moment something needs attention, right from your iPhone, iPad, or Mac. No accounts, no third-party servers, and your data stays private to you.</p>
<h2 id="your-home-remembered">Your Home, Remembered</h2>
<p id="p3">Apple’s Home app shows you this instant. <a href="https://pingie.com/homesnap" rel="noopener noreferrer">HomeSnap</a> shows you the whole story. It quietly captures a snapshot of your entire setup about once a day and tells you exactly what changed, room by room and device by device. A Timeline and a live Event Log let you scroll back through motion, lights, locks, and doors as they happened, so “did the garage really open at 2 a.m.?” finally has an answer.</p>
<h2 id="your-home-has-an-api">Your Home Has an API</h2>
<p id="p4">This is the part power users tend to love. Flip one switch in Settings and <a href="https://pingie.com/homesnap" rel="noopener noreferrer">HomeSnap</a> serves your home over your own Wi-Fi, off by default, LAN-only, no cloud and no account involved:</p>
<ul id="ul5"><li>A local web dashboard you can open from any laptop, phone, or tablet on your network: live status tiles, an activity heatmap, charts, and per-room and per-device pages.</li>
<li>A read-only JSON HTTP API with real endpoints for your home, rooms, accessories, scenes, automations, events, change history, health, and battery and temperature trends. Point a script, a shortcut, or a Home Assistant REST sensor at it and pull structured data out of HomeKit without touching a single Apple framework.</li>
<li>An MCP server (Model Context Protocol), so you can connect the AI assistant you already use, like Claude, and ask about your home in plain language: “which batteries will die first?”, “what happened in the garage last night?”, “which lights were on longest this week?” The assistant reads your home, read-only, over your own network.</li>
<li>Markdown and JSON exports of your home and its history, plus live updates over WebSocket for anything you want to build on top.</li>
</ul><p id="p6">Everything here is read-only and stays on your network. Nothing about your home is sent to a third party, and you can put a password on it.</p>
<h2 id="live-status-and-smarter-batteries">Live Status and Smarter Batteries</h2>
<p id="p7">The My Home tab is your home exactly as it is right now: which lights are on, which doors are open, what every sensor reads. Battery-powered devices are ranked most-urgent-first, and <a href="https://pingie.com/homesnap" rel="noopener noreferrer">HomeSnap</a> estimates how long each one will last (“about 3 weeks”) from its own history, so a dying sensor never catches you off guard.</p>
<h2 id="alerts-you-actually-want">Alerts You Actually Want</h2>
<p id="p8">Build your own notification rules: pick an accessory, pick a trigger (a light turns on, a garage door opens, a fan passes a speed you set), and get told when it happens. Add freeze and overheat temperature alerts, low-battery warnings, and quiet hours so nothing pings you overnight.</p>
<h2 id="insights-into-how-your-home-lives">Insights Into How Your Home Lives</h2>
<p id="p9">A native Insights screen shows an activity heatmap of your busiest hours, your most active devices and rooms, each light’s total on-time, and recent door and lock activity. The same numbers power a private web dashboard you can open from any device on your Wi-Fi.</p>
<h2 id="a-vault-for-your-setup-codes">A Vault for Your Setup Codes</h2>
<p id="p10"><a href="https://pingie.com/homesnap" rel="noopener noreferrer">HomeSnap</a> saves your HomeKit and Matter pairing codes so you can re-pair a device after a reset. Scan a code or type it in, and it comes back later as a scannable QR or a large, readable number, safely stored in iCloud.</p>
